## 6. Production Pull Request Governance: Merge Queues & Sign-Off Matrices

Once an autonomous agent’s modification passes all verification gates, the pipeline must govern how the code enters production.

### 6.1 The Multi-Tier Approval Matrix
Enterprise codebases enforce a tiered governance matrix based on **Change Blast Radius**:

| Risk Classification | Blast Radius Criteria | Verification Requirements | Governance Authority |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Tier 1 (Low Risk)** | Lockfile patch dependency updates (`1.2.3` to `1.2.4`); pure documentation edits; typo fixes. | 100% Green CI; Zero application code diffs; Clean vulnerability scan. | **Automated Fast-Forward Merge** (Zero human approval required). |
| **Tier 2 (Medium Risk)** | Minor dependency bumps; internal method refactoring; adding new unit tests; lint enforcement. | 100% Green CI; Mutation score > 80%; Dual-LLM pass; Zero breaking API changes. | **Squad Peer Review** (1 human software engineer sign-off required). |
| **Tier 3 (High Risk)** | Major dependency upgrades (`v2` to `v3`); database schema migrations; security policy changes; payment gateway modifications. | All Tier 2 assertions + Staging canary deployment + Performance benchmark validation. | **Staff Architect & Security Lead Review** (2 human principal sign-offs required). |

### 6.2 GitHub Merge Queues & Autonomous Concurrency Control
In high-velocity organizations, multiple autonomous agents may submit verified Pull Requests simultaneously. If two agents merge independently to `main`, their changes may interact in unexpected ways, causing the `main` branch to break.

To eliminate integration breaks, enterprise pipelines mandate **GitHub Merge Queues**:
* Autonomous PRs cannot be merged directly into `main`.
* When approved, the PR enters an automated FIFO (First-In, First-Out) Merge Queue.
* The merge queue merges PR A into a temporary integration branch, runs full enterprise integration tests, and only advances `main` if the build succeeds.
* If PR B arrives while PR A is running, the merge queue tests both PR A and PR B in speculative parallel batches, maximizing merge throughput while mathematically guaranteeing that broken code never reaches the primary trunk.

